A||N||6||NSUMEY||L4 Blues||||1||
Z||000000||NSUMEY||04-14-2001||12:26 PM||||I have a 1984 2.5 (vin R) I had a heating problem which cracked the head. I ordered a reman head and installed into the vehicle. After re-assembling everything I went to start the vehicle and it didn't have any compression. I removed the valve cover and verified the valves were moving. I then back all the rockers so all the valves were shut and of course there was compression..so I knew the problem was related to the valve train....via a source on the web ring I torque the rocker nuts to 32 ft lbs. I decided to remove the rockers and pushrods and BEHOLD all the freaking pushrods were bent!!!!I don't know what went wrong....could they have made an error inn ordering the head? I noticed that some of the L4's had regular hydrualic lifters and some had rollers....also noticed that the pushrods came in different lengths...so...how do I know what goes with what....perhaps the valve length is different between different years??? HELP!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||04-14-2001||11:19 PM||||You torqued the rocker nuts to 32 ft-lbs?!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>That's why your pushrods were bent. The nuts are supposed to be adjusted so that both valves are closed when that paticular cylinder is at TDC on its compression stroke. I can't explain the correct adjustment procedure for a Duke, though. Maybe someone else can help? <BR>As for differences in parts, I believe that roller lifters require shorter pushrods, but if you didn't change lifters, then your pushrods should have been the correct length.<BR>BTW, welcome to the Forum!<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 04-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||04-15-2001||01:51 AM||||Actually he's not that far off on the rocker arm bolt torque.  Both the Haynes book and the factory shop manual list the torque as either 20 (1984-1985) or 24 foot lbs (1986-1988) depending on year.  The rocker arm pivots on the Duke are not like the ones on the V-6.  They are a shouldered bolt that has to be bottomed out and torqued.  The pushrod length is what determines the proper setting on the lifters.  If pushrods for a non-roller engine are installed in a roller lifter engine, they will hold the valves open and will probably get bent the first time the engine is turned over.  Pushrod length on the Duke is critical!!!<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Rodrv6 (edited 04-15-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||04-15-2001||03:32 AM||||87-88 had roller lifters. Different cam, different rods.<P>Cough up the money and buy a repair manual for your Fiero! Relying on the web for torque specs is dangerous as you can now attest to!||||reg||11
Z||000004||theogre||04-15-2001||01:02 PM||||Roller lifters were available in 86. maybe 85. Since many 84's and maybe 85's had the engines replaced it is entirly posible they have roller cam setups.||||reg||1
Z||000005||MrPBody||04-15-2001||03:31 PM||||I recently installed a Dover rebuild head on my '85 SE.  Both this engine and my spare '85 have roller tappets.  As I understand it, '84's did not originally have rollers; however your engine may have been replaced.<P>According to Dover's on-line reference ( <A HREF="http://www.doverheads.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.doverheads.com</A> ) all '84 - '86 VIN R 2.5's use the same head (. . .767).<P>Sounds like wrong pushrod length.  Did you replace pushrods?  I can't imagine the head was milled so much as to cause valve-piston interference.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||NSUMEY||04-16-2001||09:29 AM||||Thanks for the info....I did not replace or change the pushrods....The valves never hit the top of the pistons to cause the bending....the engine must not be a 84 because it does have roller lifters...as far as I can tell...the valve springs and the valve lengths are different...what cause the bending was that the valve springs were fully compress and the lifters were bottomed out.....I still have to determine what year of head fits my block....does anyone know where to locate the casting code on the block to determine the actual year???||||reg||1
